 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(\max(|dx|, |dy|, |dz|))$. The number of iterations is proportional to the length of the longest axis projection [S8, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space. Only a few integer variables are required [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Significantly reduces positional computation time compared to floating-point methods but sacrifices grid flexibility [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Grid Requirements:** Requires the ray's origin and destination points to be located exactly at the center of volume cells [S8, unofficial]. Requires all grid cells to have constant length along each axis direction; cells need not be cubic but must be uniform per axis [S8, unofficial]. Cannot be applied to arbitrary or irregular grids; strictly requires pre-constrained, regularly spaced input data [S8, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Extremely fast, no floating-point operations (in the integer variant), low memory footprint [S8, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Does not guarantee visiting every voxel the ray physically intersects (topological incompleteness compared to DDA/Amanatides-Woo). Produces 18/26-connected lines by default, which may be undesirable for certain rendering or voxelization tasks requiring 6-connectivity.
-*   **When to use:** When performance is critical and the grid is sparse or the line is short. Suitable for simple voxel games or when approximate traversal is acceptable.
-*   **When not to use:** When strict topological continuity (6-connectivity) is required, or when the ray must visit every voxel it touches (e.g., precise volume rendering where missing a thin voxel causes artifacts). In such cases, use [Amanatides-Woo Voxel Traversal](3132-amanatides-woo-voxel-traversal.md) or [Integer Bresenham Voxelization with 6-Connectivity](10219-integer-bresenham-voxelization-with-6-connectivity.md).
+*   **Limitations:** Does not guarantee visiting every voxel the ray physically intersects (topological incompleteness compared to DDA/Amanatides-Woo) [S1, unofficial]. Produces 18- or 26-connected lines by default, which may be undesirable for certain rendering or voxelization tasks requiring 6-connectivity [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Suitable for simple voxel games or when approximate traversal is acceptable [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When strict topological continuity (6-connectivity) is required, or when the ray must visit every voxel it touches (e.g., precise volume rendering where missing a thin voxel causes artifacts) [S1, unofficial]. In such cases, use [Amanatides-Woo Voxel Traversal](3132-amanatides-woo-voxel-traversal.md) or [Integer Bresenham Voxelization with 6-Connectivity](10219-integer-bresenham-voxelization-with-6-connectivity.md).
 
 ## Background
-The 3D Bresenham algorithm is an extension of Bresenham's line algorithm, originally developed for 2D rasterization. The 3D version presented by Liu and Cheng [S5, unofficial] follows the original Bresenham algorithm closely, introducing a second error term to handle the third dimension. It is widely used in voxel-based games and simple 3D rasterization tasks due to its integer-only arithmetic.
